    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d301">

                <pb facs="0385" n="371" sameAs="frus:frus1944v06/pg371"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/15309</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gauss</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 2260</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-03-02">March 2,
                        1944</date>.<lb/>[Received March 20.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to enclose a copy of
                    despatch no. 41 of February 13, 1944, from the Secretary on detail at
                        Sian<anchor n="92" xml:id="d301-f1"/> reporting observations by a foreign
                    observer on conditions between Sian and Pingliang (east Kansu) with reference to
                    Kuomintang–Communist relations.</p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Summary</hi>. Conditions during the first part of
                        February were said to be easier than at the beginning of the year and much
                        Jess anxiety was evident among the residents of the area between Sian and
                        Pingliang. The informant noted very few troop movements and Chinese military
                        officers in that area gave no indications of any planned military operations
                        against the Chinese Communists. <hi rendition="#italic">End of Summary.</hi>
                    </p>
                </quote>
                <p>In a telegram dated February 25 Mr. Drumright states that an American who had
                    just arrived at Sian by highway from Chungking reported seeing thousands of
                    Chinese soldiers on the march both north and south between Kwang-yuan (north
                    Szechuan) and Paochi (west Shensi). This informant stated that the number of
                    southbound troops appeared to be larger than that of the troops moving north,
                    and that the former seemed to be in better physical condition than the
                    northbound group. The condition of all the troops was described, however, as
                    being very poor.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>C. E. <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
